{"chunk_id": "46f7276324a5", "text": "Bhishma recounts to Yudhishthira the famous tale of King Sivi (here associated with Vrishadarbha) to illustrate the supreme duty of protecting those who seek refuge. A pigeon fleeing a hawk takes shelter on the king\u2019s lap. When the hawk demands its natural prey, the king refuses to abandon one who has sought protection and instead offers his own flesh equal to the pigeon\u2019s weight. Piece by piece he cuts his body, yet the pigeon outweighs the flesh until the king finally climbs onto the scale himself. His uncompromising righteousness shakes the cosmos, and the gods appear, honor him, and grant him heaven. Bhishma uses the story to teach that a ruler\u2019s highest virtue is steadfast protection of dependents. The chapter then turns to another principle of kingship: honoring learned Brahmanas, whose spiritual power sustains and stabilizes the kingdom.\nKing Sivi sacrifices his own flesh to save a pigeon from a hawk, proving perfect kingship; Bhishma then teaches Yudhishthira that honoring Brahmanas is a king\u2019s foremost duty.\nThemes: self-sacrifice, royal duty, protection of supplicants, dharma of kings, reverence for Brahmanas", "vector": [0.009885242208838463, -0.021534990519285202, 0.0015230558346956968, 0.009724833071231842, -0.008421504870057106, 0.02879352495074272, -0.020679472014307976, -0.018059449270367622, -0.010767495259642601, -0.028392501175403595, 0.007826652377843857, -0.00515315867960453, 0.023967869579792023, 0.007004552986472845, -0.010159275494515896, 0.00447476003319025, 0.04175996780395508, -0.00975825171917677, 0.019636809825897217, -0.01771189458668232, -0.011108365841209888, 0.00597860012203455, 0.004879125859588385, 0.011997302062809467, -0.004047000780701637, 0.010693973861634731, 0.014730949886143208, -0.012939708307385445, 0.026013093069195747, -0.01203740481287241, -0.00711817666888237, 0.003109607147052884, 0.005069612059742212, -0.012217865325510502, -0.03130660951137543, 0.0125587359